{"_type": "issue", "id": "ema_vwap-4ix.4", "title": "Execute close-derived signals at the next tradable price", "description": "Strategy signals use the current candle close (including close-based crossover and pullback conditions), but BacktestEngine currently enters at that same close. This can introduce lookahead because the close is known only after the candle ends. Define and implement an explicit next-bar execution contract, including reversal behavior, open gaps, stop/target evaluation on the entry bar, and pending signals at end of data.", "acceptance_criteria": "- [ ] Specify when close-derived signals become executable and how entry fills are priced\\n- [ ] Ensure stop/target levels use only information available when the order is placed\\n- [ ] Define same-bar entry and stop/target precedence without future-data leakage\\n- [ ] Apply the same timing contract to signal reversals and long/short entries\\n- [ ] Add deterministic tests for gaps, reversals, and final-bar signals", "notes": "Execution contract implemented: each close-derived signal becomes executable at the next candle open; entry stop/target levels use VWAP/ATR from the signal candle; opening gaps through existing stop/target take precedence over reversals; reversals close and reopen at the next open; new entries evaluate the bar range with stop-first precedence if both levels touch; the last candle's signal remains unfilled. Added deterministic tests for entry timing, prior-candle indicators, same-bar ambiguity, gaps, reversals, both directions, and final-bar signals. Validation: full suite 364 passed, 2 skipped; diagnostics and diff check clean.", "status": "closed", "priority": 1, "issue_type": "bug", "assignee": "Hermes Agent", "owner": "hermes-agent-nous-research@users.noreply.github.com", "created_at": "2026-10-01T01:58:59Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T02:09:18Z", "started_at": "2026-10-01T02:03:37Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-01T02:09:18Z", "close_reason": "Implemented and validated next-open execution semantics with causal indicator data and deterministic event precedence.", "dependencies": [{"issue_id": "ema_vwap-4ix.4", "depends_on_id": "ema_vwap-4ix", "type": "parent-child", "created_at": "2026-10-01T01:58:59Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "metadata": "{}"}], "dependency_count": 0, "dependent_count": 0, "comment_count": 0}
{"_type": "issue", "id": "ema_vwap-4ix.1", "title": "Backtest math audit: drawdown, Sharpe, and equity-curve consistency", "description": "Audit the core performance math in the backtest engine: drawdown calculations, mark-to-market equity, annualization assumptions, Sharpe/Sortino/Calmar definitions, and degenerate-input guardrails. Ensure the metrics are mathematically consistent with the underlying equity curve and trade log, and fix any mismatch between reported curves and summary statistics.", "acceptance_criteria": "- [ ] Confirm drawdown calculation includes the opening capital reference and guards zero/negative peaks\\n- [ ] Review Sharpe/Sortino annualization using actual bar cadence and median interval assumptions\\n- [ ] Verify Calmar and drawdown-penalized metrics use compatible units\\n- [ ] Ensure equity_curve and final_equity represent the same realized and mtm state\\n- [ ] Add regression tests for degenerate and edge-case scenarios", "notes": "Implementation started: metric calculations now include initial capital baseline; Sharpe includes first-period return; Sortino uses downside RMS over all return periods; Ulcer index includes opening capital; Calmar uses annualized geometric return over fractional drawdown; non-positive peaks are guarded; empty-input result supplies all metrics. Added regression coverage for these contracts and final equity-curve consistency. Validation: pytest tests/test_backtester.py -q (6 passed); pytest tests/test_new_metrics.py tests/test_optimizer.py -q (19 passed); diagnostics clean. Task remains in progress pending broader audit/review.\nAcceptance criteria completed. Final validation: pytest tests/test_backtester.py tests/test_new_metrics.py tests/test_optimizer.py -q (26 passed); get_errors clean; git diff --check clean.", "status": "closed", "priority": 1, "issue_type": "task", "assignee": "Hermes Agent", "owner": "hermes-agent-nous-research@users.noreply.github.com", "created_at": "2026-10-01T01:46:24Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T01:53:23Z", "started_at": "2026-10-01T01:49:56Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-01T01:53:23Z", "close_reason": "Corrected baseline-aware risk metrics, asset-aware annualization, Calmar units, and empty/non-positive equity edge cases with regression coverage.", "dependencies": [{"issue_id": "ema_vwap-4ix.1", "depends_on_id": "ema_vwap-4ix", "type": "parent-child", "created_at": "2026-10-01T01:46:24Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "metadata": "{}"}], "dependency_count": 0, "dependent_count": 0, "comment_count": 0}
{"_type": "issue", "id": "ema_vwap-4rn.4", "title": "Base Gate 3 Monte Carlo on OOS equity-relative results", "description": "## Why\n`StrategyValidator` currently passes the full-sample Gate 1 trade list into Monte Carlo. The simulator resamples fixed-dollar `pnl`, even though the backtest sizes positions from current equity. With fewer than two trades, it returns zero ruin probability and zero drawdown with zero simulations; the validator can accept that as a safe result. A Monte Carlo exception also leaves zero-valued risk metrics that can earn full score unless outcome handling is corrected.\n\n## Relevant code\n- `src/validator.py`: Gate 3 input selection, thresholds, exception path, metrics, and scoring.\n- `src/wfo.py`: OOS window results and combined OOS trade data. This task depends on child `ema_vwap-4rn.3` exposing a stable chronological OOS result stream.\n- `src/monte_carlo.py`: input representation, sampling, equity path, drawdown/ruin calculation, RNG, and result fields.\n- `src/backtester.py` / `src/risk_manager.py`: realized trade PnL, fees, and equity-based position sizing.\n- `tests/test_monte_carlo.py` and `tests/test_validator.py`.\n\n## Required work\n1. Use only OOS trade/return evidence from the completed WFO windows for Gate 3. Preserve chronological ordering and exclude warm-up and IS observations.\n2. Simulate returns relative to account equity (or an equivalent well-defined R-multiple plus position-risk sizing) and reapply returns to evolving simulated equity. Do not treat fixed-dollar historical PnLs or `Trade.pnl_pct` (which is relative to position value) as account returns without conversion.\n3. Define the risk event precisely. The current rule combines a 50% peak-to-trough drawdown with ending equity at or below 50% of initial capital; document whether both conditions represent the same event or should be separate metrics. Align result/UI wording with the chosen definition.\n4. Make insufficient OOS trade count, invalid outcomes, and simulation exceptions explicit unavailable/non-passing states. Never default unavailable risk to 0% or award full score. Validate finite inputs and positive initial capital.\n5. Use a local seeded random generator rather than mutating NumPy global RNG state. Preserve reproducibility for a configured seed.\n6. Profile the simulation at realistic trade/simulation sizes; if the nested loop is material, use bounded batches/vectorization and record a before/after benchmark without allocating an unbounded simulation-by-trade matrix.\n\n## Tests\nAdd controlled outcomes that verify OOS-only sample selection, chronological returns, equity scaling, fee inclusion, the exact drawdown/ruin event, insufficient sample and invalid-input behavior, deterministic seeding, and no global RNG side effect. Add a validator test proving missing/failed Monte Carlo evidence cannot pass or receive full points. Add a repeatable benchmark or documented profiling result if implementation changes the sampling algorithm.", "design": "Consume a WFO-produced chronological OOS trade or per-period return stream, not Gate 1 trades. Prefer storing account-equity-relative realized return at the point of each trade (or enough risk/equity metadata to derive it) and compound it over each simulated path. Keep the simulation core independent of presentation. Return an explicit simulation status and sample size. Use `numpy.random.Generator`; batch large work if measurement justifies it.", "acceptance_criteria": "- [ ] Gate 3 receives OOS-only evidence from WFO, with warm-up/IS trades excluded and OOS chronology preserved.\n- [ ] Simulated outcomes scale with evolving account equity consistently with the documented position-sizing model and include net costs.\n- [ ] Ruin/drawdown event definitions and user-visible metric names are unambiguous and tested.\n- [ ] Insufficient trades, invalid data, or simulation errors are explicitly unavailable/non-passing and cannot appear as zero risk or earn full score.\n- [ ] Seeded runs are reproducible without changing global NumPy RNG state.\n- [ ] Tests cover provenance, sizing, risk event, low sample, invalid input, deterministic behavior, and validator score/status handling.\n- [ ] Simulation performance is profiled; any vectorization is bounded in memory and supported by a reproducible benchmark.", "notes": "Parent epic: `ema_vwap-4rn`. Depends on WFO child `ema_vwap-4rn.3` for trustworthy OOS data. Coordinate final threshold and unavailable-result semantics with child `ema_vwap-4rn.1`.", "status": "closed", "priority": 1, "issue_type": "task", "assignee": "Hermes Agent", "owner": "hermes-agent-nous-research@users.noreply.github.com", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:59:28Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T01:36:52Z", "started_at": "2026-10-01T01:36:51Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-01T01:36:52Z", "close_reason": "Closed", "dependencies": [{"issue_id": "ema_vwap-4rn.4", "depends_on_id": "ema_vwap-4rn", "type": "parent-child", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:59:28Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "metadata": "{}"}, {"issue_id": "ema_vwap-4rn.4", "depends_on_id": "ema_vwap-4rn.3", "type": "blocks", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:59:28Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "metadata": "{}"}], "dependency_count": 1, "dependent_count": 0, "comment_count": 0}
{"_type": "issue", "id": "ema_vwap-4rn.3", "title": "Correct Gate 2 WFE and OOS return definitions", "description": "## Why\nThe Gate 2 UI describes walk-forward efficiency as OOS Sharpe / IS Sharpe, but `WalkForwardEngine.run()` calculates the ratio of mean total-return percentages. Its branches assign 100% when average IS return is negative and OOS is nonnegative, and 0% for other nonpositive-IS cases. The validator labels `avg_out_of_sample_return_pct` as `oos_total_return_pct` and applies the minimum OOS return threshold to that average. These values do not implement the documented meaning and can mischaracterize performance across unequal window lengths.\n\n## Relevant code\n- `src/wfo.py`: window backtests, `is_sharpe`/`oos_sharpe`, `is_returns`/`oos_returns`, WFE calculation, window output, and aggregate return fields.\n- `src/validator.py`: Gate 2 metric extraction and threshold checks.\n- `src/web/index.html`: WFE help text and threshold labels.\n- `tests/test_wfo.py`, `tests/test_validator.py`, and API/UI tests as appropriate.\n\n## Required work\n1. Implement WFE according to the public contract: use comparable annualized IS/OOS Sharpe measurements, explicitly define how per-window values are aggregated, and remove arbitrary 100%/0% substitutions for negative or near-zero IS performance. Undefined or insufficient evidence must be represented explicitly and cannot pass the WFE threshold silently.\n2. Report average OOS window return under an accurate name. Separately calculate and expose a sequential aggregate OOS return across the contiguous, non-overlapping OOS windows; use the documented aggregate for `min_oos_return_pct`.\n3. Preserve window-level IS/OOS returns and Sharpe values so users can audit the aggregate and tests can verify its construction. Keep names/types consistent between `WalkForwardEngine`, `StrategyValidator`, serialized reports, and the UI.\n4. Confirm OOS windows remain contiguous and non-overlapping and that warm-up history is not counted as OOS performance.\n\n## Tests\nAdd deterministic tests using controlled window results for positive, negative, zero, and near-zero IS Sharpe; different IS/OOS durations; unequal window returns; and no valid windows. Assert the exact WFE aggregation and aggregate return formula, the metric names in the validation report, and the Gate 2 pass/caution/fail behavior at threshold boundaries.", "design": "Honor the existing UI contract of OOS Sharpe divided by IS Sharpe. Use paired per-window annualized Sharpe values and select/document a robust aggregation across windows (median is a candidate); require a meaningful positive IS denominator or mark the ratio unavailable, rather than fabricating 100% or 0%. Because OOS windows are contiguous and non-overlapping, compute the sequential aggregate return from their return factors, and retain mean window return as a separate diagnostic. Coordinate metric names with Gate 3 so the OOS trade/return stream can be reused.", "acceptance_criteria": "- [ ] WFE is calculated from the documented annualized Sharpe quantities, with its window aggregation method stated in code/API/UI documentation and tested.\n- [ ] Zero, negative, and near-zero IS Sharpe have explicit non-arbitrary handling and cannot turn poor or undefined evidence into 100% WFE.\n- [ ] Average per-window OOS return and sequential aggregate OOS return are distinct, accurately named metrics.\n- [ ] `min_oos_return_pct` checks the chosen aggregate OOS return, not an unlabeled average.\n- [ ] Window-level values remain auditable; warm-up bars are excluded from OOS results.\n- [ ] Deterministic tests cover formula cases, unequal window lengths/returns, edge cases, and threshold decisions.", "notes": "Parent epic: `ema_vwap-4rn`. This task should produce the OOS trades or return series needed by child `ema_vwap-4rn.4` (Monte Carlo); expose them in a stable internal report/result field and avoid sending an unnecessarily large trade list to browser clients.", "status": "closed", "priority": 1, "issue_type": "task", "assignee": "Hermes Agent", "owner": "hermes-agent-nous-research@users.noreply.github.com", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:57:13Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T01:36:44Z", "started_at": "2026-10-01T01:36:43Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-01T01:36:44Z", "close_reason": "Closed", "dependencies": [{"issue_id": "ema_vwap-4rn.3", "depends_on_id": "ema_vwap-4rn", "type": "parent-child", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:57:13Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "metadata": "{}"}], "dependency_count": 0, "dependent_count": 1, "comment_count": 0}
{"_type": "issue", "id": "ema_vwap-4rn.2", "title": "Correct Gate 1 equity drawdown and final-exit costs", "description": "## Why\nGate 1 uses `BacktestEngine` equity analytics. The equity curve records the first candle only after any entry fee and mark-to-market, so max drawdown can omit loss from initial capital to that first recorded value. Also, normal stock exits charge SEC and FINRA fees, while the end-of-data close path appears to charge only taker fee. Both can understate the risk or overstate performance used by Gate 1 and later Monte Carlo.\n\n## Relevant code\n- `src/backtester.py`: equity curve recording, max drawdown calculation, normal exit fee calculation, and final end-of-data liquidation.\n- `src/validator.py`: Gate 1 consumes total return, Sharpe, and max drawdown.\n- `tests/test_backtester.py` and `tests/test_validator.py`.\n\n## Required work\n1. Include initial account equity as the opening peak for drawdown calculations, while preserving a coherent timestamp/equity-curve contract for API and chart consumers.\n2. Apply the same applicable stock regulatory fees to end-of-data exits as to normal exits; avoid charging the entry fee twice and keep recorded trade fees/PnL consistent.\n3. Verify the equity curve, final equity, net profit, return, max drawdown, and trade PnL reconcile for a first-bar loss and for a stock position liquidated at the final bar.\n\n## Tests\nAdd deterministic backtester tests where a fee or first-bar adverse move makes drawdown exceed the value computed from the first post-entry point, and where end-of-data stock liquidation includes SEC/FINRA fees. Assert reconciliation among trade-level PnL/fees, final equity, total return, and drawdown.", "design": "Preserve existing backtest API outputs unless an equity curve contract change is necessary; if the opening-capital point is added, verify chart consumers tolerate it. Reuse a shared exit-fee calculation so normal and end-of-data liquidation cannot drift. Include all costs in realized PnL and in equity before calculating performance statistics.", "acceptance_criteria": "- [ ] Max drawdown is measured from at least initial capital and includes initial execution costs and losses.\n- [ ] End-of-data stock liquidation applies the same applicable regulatory fee rules as a normal exit.\n- [ ] Final equity, net profit, total return, equity curve, trade PnL, and fee totals reconcile without double charging.\n- [ ] Focused deterministic tests cover first-recorded-point drawdown and stock end-of-data fees; existing backtester and validator tests pass.", "notes": "Parent epic: `ema_vwap-4rn`. This task corrects Gate 1 inputs consumed by all downstream validation, not just the displayed Gate 1 metrics.", "status": "closed", "priority": 1, "issue_type": "task", "assignee": "Hermes Agent", "owner": "hermes-agent-nous-research@users.noreply.github.com", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:55:18Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T01:29:01Z", "started_at": "2026-10-01T01:26:58Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-01T01:29:01Z", "close_reason": "Closed", "dependencies": [{"issue_id": "ema_vwap-4rn.2", "depends_on_id": "ema_vwap-4rn", "type": "parent-child", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:55:18Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "metadata": "{}"}], "dependency_count": 0, "dependent_count": 0, "comment_count": 0}
{"_type": "issue", "id": "ema_vwap-4rn.1", "title": "Wire validation thresholds and align gate outcomes", "description": "## Why\nThe Validation Gates settings are saved and displayed, but `validateStrategyConfig()` does not include `criteria` in its POST body. `validate_strategy_endpoint()` therefore constructs default `ValidationCriteria` for normal UI requests. Separately, a gate can have `status=CAUTION` and `passed=True`, so the `gates_passed` count can describe a caution result as a pass. A Gate 3 exception leaves risk metrics at zero, allowing the score calculation to award full risk points.\n\n## Relevant code\n- `src/web/app.js`: `validateStrategyConfig()`, existing settings load/save, and settings control IDs.\n- `src/app.py`: `ValidateStrategyRequest` and `/api/strategy/validate`.\n- `src/validator.py`: `ValidationCriteria`, status transitions, pass count, overall status, and score synthesis.\n- Existing tests: `tests/test_validator.py` and API/frontend tests as appropriate.\n\n## Required work\n1. Pass the currently configured threshold values using the `ValidationCriteria` field names when the UI starts validation. Preserve API default behavior when an external caller omits `criteria`.\n2. Verify values persist/load consistently and resolve duplicate DOM IDs or stale cross-wired fields that cause a threshold to read from the wrong control.\n3. Define and enforce one contract for PASS, CAUTION, FAIL, unavailable, `passed`, `gates_passed`, overall status, and score. A caution must not be counted as an unqualified pass; unavailable/exception metrics must not be interpreted as zero risk or earn full Gate 3 points.\n4. Validate threshold input ranges at the API/configuration boundary where appropriate, with messages identifying the invalid field.\n\n## Tests\nAdd focused API or unit tests proving that non-default submitted criteria affect their corresponding gate decisions, omitted criteria use defaults, threshold values survive settings load/save and request construction, invalid criteria are rejected, and PASS/CAUTION/FAIL/unavailable reports have coherent counts and scores.", "design": "Use the existing `ValidationCriteria` dataclass field names as the request contract. Keep API defaults for older clients. Prefer `gates_passed` to count only gates whose final status is PASS; represent CAUTION and unavailable results distinctly and ensure overall status derives from the individual outcomes. Scores may remain partial for measured caution results, but missing or failed measurements must not receive full credit.", "acceptance_criteria": "- [ ] UI-triggered validation sends all supported ValidationCriteria values and the server applies them to the run.\n- [ ] Requests without criteria remain backward-compatible and use documented defaults.\n- [ ] Duplicate or cross-wired settings controls do not cause one field to overwrite another.\n- [ ] `passed`, gate status, `gates_passed`, overall status, and score follow a documented consistent policy.\n- [ ] Gate exceptions and unavailable metrics cannot be treated as zero risk or receive full risk points.\n- [ ] Focused tests cover criteria propagation, defaults, validation errors, and outcome semantics.", "notes": "Related prior work: closed `ema_vwap-tei` added threshold controls and API criteria support, but the current frontend validation request still omits the criteria payload. Parent epic: `ema_vwap-4rn`.", "status": "closed", "priority": 1, "issue_type": "task", "assignee": "Hermes Agent", "owner": "hermes-agent-nous-research@users.noreply.github.com", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:54:12Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T01:40:23Z", "started_at": "2026-10-01T01:06:53Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-01T01:40:23Z", "close_reason": "Closed", "dependencies": [{"issue_id": "ema_vwap-4rn.1", "depends_on_id": "ema_vwap-4rn", "type": "parent-child", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:54:12Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "metadata": "{}"}], "dependency_count": 0, "dependent_count": 0, "comment_count": 0}
{"_type": "issue", "id": "ema_vwap-4rn", "title": "Harden the three-gate strategy validation system", "description": "## Why\nA review of `src/validator.py`, `src/wfo.py`, `src/monte_carlo.py`, and `src/backtester.py` found that several gate labels do not match their implemented metrics, risk simulation is based on in-sample fixed-dollar PnL, and validation requests do not use the configured UI thresholds. The existing validation tests pass but do not pin the mathematical definitions or these failure cases.\n\n## Current findings\n- The UI validation request omits `criteria`; the API constructs default `ValidationCriteria`, so edits in the Validation Gates settings do not control validation. The closed threshold-exposure issue `ema_vwap-tei` did not resolve this runtime request wiring.\n- Gate 1 drawdown starts from the first recorded equity point rather than explicitly including starting capital. Stock end-of-data liquidation applies taker fees but appears to omit the regulatory fees charged on normal exits.\n- Gate 2 documents WFE as OOS Sharpe divided by IS Sharpe but calculates a ratio of average total-return percentages. It assigns arbitrary values for nonpositive IS returns. The field `avg_out_of_sample_return_pct` is consumed as `oos_total_return_pct`, and the minimum OOS return gate therefore checks an average window return rather than a clearly defined aggregate.\n- Gate 3 simulates the full-sample Gate 1 trade list as fixed-dollar PnL, reports zero risk when fewer than two trades prevent simulation, and can award full risk points when simulation errors occur.\n- Several CAUTION outcomes leave `passed=True`; gate counts and scores can therefore disagree with the displayed gate status.\n\n## Scope\nCorrect and test the configured-threshold path, outcome semantics, Gate 1 account-risk calculations, Gate 2 metric definitions and OOS aggregation, and Gate 3 OOS risk simulation. Add focused tests that establish the chosen behavior, including degenerate and failed-simulation cases. Preserve current API compatibility where practical and document intentional behavior changes.\n\n## Out of scope\nDo not retune default financial thresholds or claim that passing these historical tests guarantees live profitability. Do not redesign the strategy, optimizer, deployment process, or portfolio risk engine as part of this work.", "design": "Use the current UI/API contract as the starting point and make each metric definition explicit. For WFE, follow the documented OOS Sharpe / IS Sharpe meaning unless a product decision intentionally changes the contract; define aggregation across windows and guard zero or negative denominators without assigning arbitrary 100% efficiency. Report average per-window OOS return separately from compounded/aggregate OOS return. Preserve per-trade OOS account-relative returns (or an equivalent return-on-equity/R-multiple measure) so Monte Carlo can rescale outcomes as simulated equity changes. A CAUTION or unavailable result must not count as an unqualified PASS; missing/failed risk calculations must not be scored as zero risk.", "acceptance_criteria": "- [ ] Every exposed Validation Gates threshold used by the UI is sent to the validation API and applied to that run; API callers that omit criteria retain documented defaults.\n- [ ] Gate status, `passed`, `gates_passed`, overall status, and score agree for PASS, CAUTION, FAIL, and unavailable/error outcomes; unavailable metrics never receive full risk points.\n- [ ] Gate 1 drawdown includes starting equity and all applicable fees, including stock regulatory fees on end-of-data exits.\n- [ ] Gate 2 WFE definition matches its public help text, has explicit behavior for zero/negative IS performance, and does not silently substitute average window return for aggregate OOS return.\n- [ ] Gate 3 uses OOS evidence, models equity-relative trade outcomes consistently with position sizing, and reports insufficient evidence as unavailable/non-passing rather than zero risk.\n- [ ] Focused deterministic tests cover formulas, thresholds, gate status/count/score consistency, low-sample and error cases, stock final-exit costs, and OOS trade provenance.\n- [ ] Existing validator, WFO, Monte Carlo, and backtester tests pass; API/UI behavior and metric names are documented where changed.", "notes": "Implementation is decomposed into children: (1) criteria wiring and report semantics, (2) Gate 1 accounting, (3) Gate 2 WFE/OOS definitions, (4) Gate 3 OOS-based Monte Carlo. Gate 3 depends on Gate 2 exposing the OOS trade stream or equivalent OOS return series. Relevant baseline: `pytest tests/test_validator.py tests/test_wfo.py tests/test_monte_carlo.py -q` currently passes 24 tests, but those tests do not establish these contracts.", "status": "closed", "priority": 1, "issue_type": "epic", "owner": "hermes-agent-nous-research@users.noreply.github.com", "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:52:37Z", "created_by": "Hermes Agent", "updated_at": "2026-10-01T01:40:24Z", "closed_at": "2026-10-01T01:40:24Z", "close_reason": "Closed", "dependency_count": 0, "dependent_count": 0, "comment_count": 0}
